# The CHSH game and the Bell inequality, the exact discrete bound that quantum entanglement violates, modeled and PROVED
# by the kernel. Two players, Alice and Bob, are placed far apart and cannot talk once the game starts. A referee hands
# Alice a question bit x and Bob a question bit y. Each answers with one bit, Alice with a and Bob with b. They WIN the
# round when a XOR b equals x AND y. The four question pairs (off, off), (off, on), (on, off), (on, on) are asked equally
# often, so the score is the count of pairs won out of four. Because the players are separated, any classical recipe is a
# pair of LOCAL strategies: Alice's answer depends only on x, Bob's only on y, with no shared question. There are exactly
# four single-bit functions (constant off, constant on, identity, flip), so sixteen strategy pairs in all. This file
# CONSTRUCTS the strategies, COMPUTES the winning predicate and the win count over the four questions, and PROVES the
# headline Bell / CHSH bound: NO pair of local strategies wins all four rounds, so the win count is at most three. The
# best classical play scores three of four, a win rate of three quarters, and that is exactly tight. This three-quarters
# ceiling is what a theory of LOCAL HIDDEN VARIABLES (predetermined answers fixed in advance) can never beat. Quantum
# players sharing an entangled pair (a Bell state from the companion file) reach a win rate near eighty-five percent, the
# Tsirelson bound, beating three quarters. That excess is Bell's theorem: the measured correlations of entangled qubits
# cannot be reproduced by any local recipe, so no local hidden variable model can underlie quantum mechanics. The win
# count here is a Peano natural and the bound is the kernel's at-most order, so the whole statement reduces and checks.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag

# A bit, the value of a question or an answer, off or on.
form bit
  case off
  case on

# Exclusive or of two bits, on exactly when the two differ. This is the left side of the winning condition: the parity
# of Alice's answer against Bob's answer.
task exclusive-or
  take a, like bit
  take b, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, read a
    case off
      send back
        read b
    case on
      fork case, read b
        case off
          send back
            make on
        case on
          send back
            make off

# Conjunction of two bits, on exactly when both are on. This is the right side of the winning condition: the product of
# the two question bits, the target parity the answers must match.
task and-bit
  take a, like bit
  take b, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, read a
    case off
      send back
        make off
    case on
      send back
        read b

# Equality of two bits, yes when they match. Used to compare the answer parity against the target.
task same-bit
  take a, like bit
  take b, like bit
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case off
      fork case, read b
        case off
          send back
            make yes
        case on
          send back
            make no
    case on
      fork case, read b
        case off
          send back
            make no
        case on
          send back
            make yes

# A local strategy: one of the four single-bit functions a separated player can use. With no shared question, a player's
# answer is a fixed function of their own question alone, and there are exactly these four. `low` always answers off,
# `high` always answers on, `copy` echoes the question, and `swap` flips it.
form strategy
  case low
  case high
  case copy
  case swap

# Apply a strategy to a question bit to get the answer bit. This is the whole content of a local player: a lookup with no
# access to the other side's question.
task apply-strategy
  take s, like strategy
  take q, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, read s
    case low
      send back
        make off
    case high
      send back
        make on
    case copy
      send back
        read q
    case swap
      fork case, read q
        case off
          send back
            make on
        case on
          send back
            make off

# Whether a strategy pair wins the round on questions x and y: the parity of the two answers equals the product of the
# two questions. wins alice bob x y = ( (apply alice x) XOR (apply bob y) == x AND y ). This is the CHSH winning rule
# written out exactly.
task wins
  take alice, like strategy
  take bob, like strategy
  take x, like bit
  take y, like bit
  like flag
  send back
    call same-bit
      call exclusive-or
        call apply-strategy
          read alice
          read x
        call apply-strategy
          read bob
          read y
      call and-bit
        read x
        read y

# The win count is a Peano natural, zero through four, counting how many of the four questions a strategy pair wins.
form natural
  case zero
  case succ
    link prior, like natural

# Score one round: a win contributes one, a loss contributes zero, added on top of the running count. This folds the
# yes / no of `wins` into the natural total.
task score
  take result, like flag
  take rest, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read result
    case yes
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            read rest
    case no
      send back
        read rest

# The win count of a strategy pair over all four questions, (off, off), (off, on), (on, off), (on, on). Each round is
# scored and added, so the result is a natural from zero to four. This is the CHSH score of a classical recipe.
task win-count
  take alice, like strategy
  take bob, like strategy
  like natural
  send back
    call score
      call wins
        read alice
        read bob
        make off
        make off
      call score
        call wins
          read alice
          read bob
          make off
          make on
        call score
          call wins
            read alice
            read bob
            make on
            make off
          call score
            call wins
              read alice
              read bob
              make on
              make on
            make zero

# at-most a b = on iff a <= b, by recursion on both naturals at once. The kernel uses this to state the Bell bound as a
# decidable inequality that reduces to `on`.
task at-most
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like bit
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        make on
    case succ
      link ap
      fork case, read b
        case zero
          send back
            make off
        case succ
          link bp
          send back
            call at-most
              read ap
              read bp

# Three, the classical CHSH ceiling, as a successor tower. The best local recipe wins exactly this many of the four rounds.
task three
  like natural
  send back
    make succ
      bind prior
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero

# THE BELL / CHSH BOUND, the headline theorem: NO pair of local strategies wins all four rounds. For EVERY Alice strategy
# and EVERY Bob strategy, the win count is at most three. at-most (win-count alice bob) three = on, for all sixteen pairs.
# Proved by exhaustive induction over both strategy enums (`fold alice, bob`): the kernel walks all sixteen pairs, in each
# one reduces the four-round win count to a concrete natural, and checks it is at most three. This is the exact ceiling a
# local hidden variable theory cannot exceed, and the one entangled quantum players beat (Tsirelson, near eighty-five
# percent), which is Bell's theorem.
rule no-local-strategy-wins-all-four
  mark alice, like strategy
  mark bob, like strategy
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        call win-count
          read alice
          read bob
        call three
      make on
  fold alice, bob

# TIGHTNESS: the bound is achieved. The pair where Alice copies her question and Bob always answers off wins exactly three
# of the four rounds, so three is not just an upper bound but the true classical optimum. win-count copy low = three.
# Witnessed by computation. Together with the bound above, the classical CHSH value is exactly three of four.
rule a-strategy-achieves-three
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call win-count
        make copy
        make low
      call three
  calm hold

# THE WINNING PREDICATE IS EXACTLY THE CHSH CONDITION on a concrete round: on questions (on, on) the target parity is on,
# so the answers must DIFFER. The pair copy / low answers on and off there, which differ, so this round is won. This pins
# down that `wins` is the rule a XOR b = x AND y and nothing else.
rule chsh-condition-on-the-hard-round
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call wins
        make copy
        make low
        make on
        make on
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE SAME HARD ROUND IS WHERE ALL-OFF FAILS: two constant-off players answer off and off, whose parity is off, but on
# questions (on, on) the target is on, so they LOSE this round. No constant pair can win all four, the simplest face of
# the bound. wins low low on on = no.
rule constant-pair-loses-the-hard-round
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call wins
        make low
        make low
        make on
        make on
      make no
  calm hold
